    title: 'EMS & NMS Architecture',
    subtitle: 'The 4-tier management hierarchy from NMS down to network elements',
    blocks: [
      { id: 'nms', label: 'NMS — Network Management System', detail: 'The NMS provides a unified management interface across all technology domains. It aggregates data from multiple EMSs to deliver end-to-end service views, cross-domain fault correlation, centralized alarm management, network-wide performance dashboards, and OSS/BSS northbound interfaces. An NMS typically integrates with 3–10 EMSs depending on the operator\'s technology diversity. Examples: Nokia NetAct, Ericsson ENM, Cisco NSO, Amdocs NMS.', emoji: '🖥️', color: 'purple' },
      { id: 'ems', label: 'EMS — Element Management System', detail: 'An EMS manages one specific technology domain (e.g., RAN EMS, Core EMS, Transport EMS). It provides FCAPS functions for all NEs in that domain. The EMS knows the detailed vendor-specific models of its NEs, translates them to a common northbound interface (typically RESTCONF or SNMP), and exposes them to the NMS. Each EMS has a dedicated southbound interface protocol for its NEs (NETCONF for modern NEs, SNMP for legacy). Multiple EMSs can be managed by a single NMS.', emoji: '📊', color: 'blue' },
      { id: 'ne', label: 'NE — Network Element', detail: 'The NE is the physical or virtual device: eNB/gNB (base stations), MME/AMF (core network functions), routers, switches, firewalls. Each NE runs management agent software (SNMP agent, NETCONF server, gNMI agent) that exposes configuration and state. NEs generate SNMP traps, syslog messages, performance counters, and streaming telemetry. In NFV environments, NEs become VNFs (Virtual Network Functions) running as VMs/containers on standard x86 hardware.', emoji: '🔧', color: 'green' },
      { id: 'sbi', label: 'SBI — Southbound Interface', detail: 'The SBI is the protocol layer through which EMS/NMS communicates with NEs. Protocols: SNMP (GET, SET, TRAP — legacy, monitoring-focused), NETCONF (transactional configuration over SSH, YANG-modelled), RESTCONF (HTTP-based NETCONF-alternative, JSON support), gNMI (gRPC-based streaming telemetry), CLI (screen-scraping, legacy/proprietary), TL1 (legacy telecom). The SBI must support multiple protocols because of multi-vendor, multi-technology networks — no single protocol is universal.', emoji: '🔌', color: 'indigo' },
      { id: 'nbi', label: 'NBI — Northbound Interface', detail: 'The NBI is the API through which the NMS exposes managed network data to OSS/BSS systems: service fulfilment (order management, provisioning), assurance (fault management, SLA monitoring), billing (mediation, CDR processing), and analytics (ML/AI pipelines). Standard NBIs: REST APIs (TMF API framework), SOAP/XML (legacy), Kafka streams (real-time events). The NBI abstracts network complexity — an OSS application sees "services" and "customers", not interfaces and VLANs.', emoji: '☁️', color: 'cyan' },
    ],
